{
  "id": "80808",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Le Châtelier's Principle",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "As we saw in the previous module, reversible reactions proceed in both directions (reactants form products and products form reactants). A system at equilibrium is in a state of dynamic balance, with forward and reverse reactions taking place at equal rates. We can tell that a reaction is at equilibrium if the reaction quotient ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is equal to the equilibrium constant ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": "). Once at equilibrium, a system will remain at equilibrium until a change is imposed on the system."
              }
            ],
            "id": "ymno7y1scald5v6",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We next address what happens when a system at equilibrium is subjected to a change in conditions (a "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "stress"
              },
              {
                "text": ") such that "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " is not equal to "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": " and the system is no longer at equilibrium. If "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " < "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the rate of the forward reaction is larger than the rate of the reverse reaction, and the system will shift toward the products until "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " = "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": " and equilibrium is reestablished. If "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " > "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the rate of the reverse reaction is larger than the rate of the forward reaction, and the system will shift toward the reactants until "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " = "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": " and equilibrium is reestablished. This phenomenon is summarized by "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Le Châtelier's principle"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An empirical framework stating that when a system at equilibrium is subjected to a stress (such as a change in concentration, pressure, or temperature), the system will adjust in a way that counteracts the stress and reestablishes equilibrium."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2895455446",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "705541",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "nx09ejheeagk7es",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "qx0ojfwg15ugu2z",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "6oshre5b1l441bf",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/A3/A31F9808B584CB71B1FEA56DD0BCDE4E/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "m4w124v7z2f9c47",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "ur0vjhkd2i6h5m6",
            "items": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "When a chemical system at equilibrium is disturbed (stressed), the disturbance causes a change in "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "Q"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ". The reaction system will then experience a shift in response to the disturbance such that equilibrium is reestablished and "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "Q"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is again equal to "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "."
                      }
                    ],
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "dd"
              }
            ],
            "title": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " Le Châtelier's Principle "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "716071704",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "type": "dl"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ygb61q8p9qa2vfw",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4h8r05o72t7va79",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/A3/A31F9808B584CB71B1FEA56DD0BCDE4E/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Le Châtelier's principle can be used to predict changes in equilibrium concentrations when a system that is at equilibrium is subjected to a stress. However, if we have a mixture of reactants and products that have not yet reached equilibrium, the changes necessary to reach equilibrium may not be so obvious. In such a case, we can compare the values of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": " for the system to predict the changes."
              }
            ],
            "id": "e9sew2jodpthnuu",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Throughout this module, we use the following reaction to illustrate Le Châtelier's principle:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "yhj1t93zxks27gc",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "j58l2totfmn5a5n",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5bwrqa3koyrxf49",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\underset{\\mathrm{pink}}{\\mathrm{Co}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_6\\;^{2+}(aq)}\\;\\;+\\;4\\;\\mathrm{Cl}^-(aq)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\; \\underset{\\mathrm{blue}}{{\\mathrm{CoCl}}_4\\;^{2-}(aq)}\\;+\\;6\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Although this is not the case for most reversible reactions, for this reaction, the changes that occur in the concentrations of products and reactants are visible through a change in color."
              }
            ],
            "id": "usjdidcwmx4kcht",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "3566668982",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "One way to make blue pigments for jewelry or paints is to use compounds that contain cobalt (Co). When cobalt ions, Co"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", and chloride ions, Cl"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", dissolve in water, cobalt is surrounded either with water molecules to form Co(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " or with chloride ions to form CoCl"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". This leads to the following reversible reaction:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2brzdhat706u1ej",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "nahhnrb2qw84l2i",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "d4veeckrek84ttd",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\underset{\\mathrm{pink}}{\\mathrm{Co}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_6\\;^{2+}(aq)}\\;\\;+\\;4\\;\\mathrm{Cl}^-(aq)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\; \\underset{\\mathrm{blue}}{{\\mathrm{CoCl}}_4\\;^{2-}(aq)}\\;+\\;6\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Co(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is pink, and CoCl"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is blue."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "u64cjloadbv69fj",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1068638870",
            "selection": {
              "anchor": {
                "offset": 0,
                "path": [
                  0,
                  0
                ]
              },
              "focus": {
                "offset": 0,
                "path": [
                  1,
